1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,150
When Singapore Had Wild Tigers

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:05,513
By the middle of the 19th century,

3
00:00:05,593 --> 00:00:09,975
tigers in Singapore were rumoured
to kill a person every day.

4
00:00:09,975 --> 00:00:13,956
Pressed on the subject in the
House of Commons, the Governor of

5
00:00:14,036 --> 00:00:18,016
the Straits Settlements put the
real figure at a still-alarming

6
00:00:18,096 --> 00:00:22,350
two hundred deaths a year, in a
population of only fifty thousand.

7
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:25,717
The last of those tigers was shot in 1930,

8
00:00:25,797 --> 00:00:29,900
at the edge of what is now
Choa Chu Kang New Town.

9
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:33,671
Wild animals never really
left Singapore after that —

10
00:00:33,751 --> 00:00:36,875
they just got smaller,
and mostly harmless.

11
00:00:36,875 --> 00:00:40,678
An otter family crossing the
road in the middle of the

12
00:00:40,758 --> 00:00:44,425
financial district now gets
a road sign of its own.

13
00:00:44,425 --> 00:00:47,132
Singapore's tigers were never native in

14
00:00:47,212 --> 00:00:50,000
the sense of having nowhere else to go.

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,225
They swam across the Strait
of Johor from Malaya —

16
00:00:53,305 --> 00:00:59,043
strong enough swimmers that some were caught
in the fishing stakes set along the shore —

17
00:00:59,123 --> 00:01:02,825
and found a virgin jungle
full of pigs and deer to hunt.

18
00:01:02,825 --> 00:01:05,933
The earliest newspaper
report of an attack,

19
00:01:06,013 --> 00:01:10,085
in the Singapore Chronicle
on the 8th of September 1831,

20
00:01:10,165 --> 00:01:13,866
described a Chinese man
killed, with the same tiger

21
00:01:13,946 --> 00:01:17,950
probably responsible for a
second death shortly after.

22
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:22,133
In May 1839 two labourers
were carried off near what

23
00:01:22,212 --> 00:01:26,475
is today Serangoon Road,
then known as Rangong Road.

24
00:01:26,475 --> 00:01:30,325
The real danger began
with gambier and pepper.

25
00:01:30,325 --> 00:01:34,390
When cultivation of the two
crops took off from the 1840s,

26
00:01:34,470 --> 00:01:39,536
plantations pushed out of the town
and into the jungle tigers hunted in;

27
00:01:39,616 --> 00:01:43,475
by the late 1840s there were
some six hundred of them.

28
00:01:43,475 --> 00:01:48,376
Chinese plantation coolies
made easy, isolated targets,

29
00:01:48,456 --> 00:01:53,800
and reports of encounters rose
through the 1830s and 1840s.

30
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:56,275
By the middle of the century tigers

31
00:01:56,355 --> 00:01:59,275
were rumoured to kill someone every day.

32
00:01:59,275 --> 00:02:02,994
William Butterworth, the Governor
of the Straits Settlements,

33
00:02:03,074 --> 00:02:06,108
questioned on the subject
in the House of Commons,

34
00:02:06,188 --> 00:02:09,925
gave a still-alarming estimate
of two hundred deaths a year.

35
00:02:09,925 --> 00:02:13,565
A village near Bukit Timah was abandoned

36
00:02:13,645 --> 00:02:17,550
altogether in 1859 after repeated attacks.

37
00:02:17,550 --> 00:02:21,136
Officially, only seven
deaths were reported to the

38
00:02:21,216 --> 00:02:25,315
police in 1857, against a
believed toll of three hundred;

39
00:02:25,395 --> 00:02:29,127
plantation owners often
kept quiet about the deaths,

40
00:02:29,207 --> 00:02:32,800
unwilling to scare off
the next batch of workers.

41
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,675
The government's answer was money.

42
00:02:35,675 --> 00:02:40,205
A reward of twenty dollars a tiger
climbed to fifty, then a hundred,

43
00:02:40,285 --> 00:02:43,053
then a hundred and fifty as the toll grew,

44
00:02:43,133 --> 00:02:47,675
and hunters could earn more again
selling the skin and other parts.

45
00:02:47,675 --> 00:02:52,188
Pits four to four and a half metres
deep were dug and camouflaged;

46
00:02:52,268 --> 00:02:57,339
a tiger caught alive went into a rattan
basket too strong to bite through,

47
00:02:57,419 --> 00:03:01,525
and one caught in a pit was
often simply shot where it lay.

48
00:03:01,525 --> 00:03:06,131
Hunting tigers became something
between a public service and a sport,

49
00:03:06,211 --> 00:03:10,625
lucrative enough that at least
two Europeans made a living at it.

50
00:03:10,625 --> 00:03:14,752
In 1859 the Superintendent
of Convicts, J. F. A. McNair,

51
00:03:14,832 --> 00:03:19,259
put convict labourers on regular
patrol through Bukit Timah,

52
00:03:19,339 --> 00:03:21,963
Serangoon, Changi and Choa Chu Kang;

53
00:03:22,043 --> 00:03:26,325
within a year the patrols had
killed half a dozen tigers.

54
00:03:26,325 --> 00:03:30,225
The bounty and the shrinking
jungle did their work.

55
00:03:30,225 --> 00:03:34,574
A man was killed by a tiger
on Thomson Road in 1890,

56
00:03:34,654 --> 00:03:39,089
and two more tigers were
shot at Bukit Timah in 1896,

57
00:03:39,169 --> 00:03:42,150
but sightings after that grew rare.

58
00:03:42,150 --> 00:03:45,789
In 1902 a tiger did turn
up in the middle of town,

59
00:03:45,869 --> 00:03:49,656
hiding under the elevated
Bar & Billiard Room of the

60
00:03:49,736 --> 00:03:53,975
Raffles Hotel — but it was
not one of the wild survivors.

61
00:03:53,975 --> 00:03:57,286
It had escaped from a
travelling circus on Beach

62
00:03:57,366 --> 00:04:00,536
Road and was shot by
Charles McGowan Phillips,

63
00:04:00,616 --> 00:04:03,725
the principal of nearby
Raffles Institution.

64
00:04:03,725 --> 00:04:08,041
It's the story most people know;
the wild tiger it's usually

65
00:04:08,121 --> 00:04:12,150
mistaken for had, by then,
almost nowhere left to hide.

66
00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:15,540
By the 1920s a wild tiger in Singapore

67
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:18,450
was rare enough to be an event.

68
00:04:18,450 --> 00:04:22,800
My wife's family has a thread
in this story of its own.

69
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:27,086
One of her maternal relatives, by
family account, was part of the

70
00:04:27,166 --> 00:04:31,654
hunting party on the 12th of August
1928 that tracked down a Malayan

71
00:04:31,734 --> 00:04:36,221
tiger off West Coast in the Pasir
Panjang district — though he never

72
00:04:36,301 --> 00:04:40,600
claimed to have fired the shot
himself, only to have been there.

73
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:43,375
That hunt is on the record.

74
00:04:43,375 --> 00:04:47,175
A group photograph held by
the National Archives names

75
00:04:47,255 --> 00:04:50,553
sixteen of the party —
Tan Tian Quee as leader,

76
00:04:50,633 --> 00:04:54,361
Tan Ah Mui credited with
the shot, a tracker recorded

77
00:04:54,441 --> 00:04:58,250
only as Mahally — with a
row of beaters left unnamed.

78
00:04:58,250 --> 00:05:03,522
The tiger was killed in the jungle at
the tenth milestone of West Coast Road,

79
00:05:03,602 --> 00:05:07,425
and measured eight feet three
inches from nose to tail.

80
00:05:07,425 --> 00:05:11,275
The same party finished
the job two years later.

81
00:05:11,275 --> 00:05:15,499
On the 26th of October 1930,
at eleven in the morning,

82
00:05:15,579 --> 00:05:19,803
on the fringe of Choa Chu
Kang village, Ong Kim Hong —

83
00:05:19,883 --> 00:05:24,825
one of the 1928 group — shot a
tiger of eight feet six inches.

84
00:05:24,825 --> 00:05:29,550
It was the last wild tiger
anyone recorded in Singapore.

85
00:05:29,550 --> 00:05:33,700
A photograph of the party under
the village's roadside sign,

86
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:36,027
several dozen men and their dogs,

87
00:05:36,107 --> 00:05:39,350
survives in the National
Library's collection.

88
00:05:39,350 --> 00:05:42,933
A century later, Singapore
has wild animals again,

89
00:05:43,013 --> 00:05:46,750
just not the kind that used
to kill a person a day.

90
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:50,846
Smooth-coated otters, extinct
here for decades, began turning up

91
00:05:50,926 --> 00:05:54,957
in the 1990s and have since
colonised the island's waterways so

92
00:05:55,037 --> 00:05:59,067
thoroughly that a family swimming
past Robertson Quay or Sungei

93
00:05:59,147 --> 00:06:02,460
Serangoon is now closer to
routine than remarkable —

94
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:07,289
routine enough that the road sign
pictured above shares a signpost with an

95
00:06:07,369 --> 00:06:11,675
ordinary traffic-slowing notice,
not a special warning of its own.

96
00:06:11,675 --> 00:06:15,209
The Oriental Pied Hornbill
tells a similar story:

97
00:06:15,289 --> 00:06:19,782
locally extinct for decades, it
recolonised Singapore from the

98
00:06:19,862 --> 00:06:24,871
1990s onward and is now seen in
parks and reserves across the island,

99
00:06:24,951 --> 00:06:27,975
from Sungei Buloh to the Botanic Gardens.

100
00:06:27,975 --> 00:06:30,975
Not every comeback is welcome.

101
00:06:30,975 --> 00:06:34,807
Wild boars from the forests
of Pulau Ubin and the Central

102
00:06:34,887 --> 00:06:39,817
Catchment area have pushed into nearby
housing estates often enough that,

103
00:06:39,897 --> 00:06:43,179
in a 2020 survey, nearly
40 per cent of Pasir Ris

104
00:06:43,259 --> 00:06:47,366
residents said they would rather
the animals — along with the

105
00:06:47,446 --> 00:06:51,975
neighbourhood's stray dogs — were
removed or relocated altogether.

106
00:06:51,975 --> 00:06:57,014
The National Parks Board's usual
first move is to relocate an animal;

107
00:06:57,094 --> 00:07:01,168
it culls only when an animal
is judged a genuine danger,

108
00:07:01,248 --> 00:07:05,471
as with a boar that attacked
a woman and bit an officer in

109
00:07:05,551 --> 00:07:10,150
Punggol in February 2021, after
residents had been feeding it.

110
00:07:10,150 --> 00:07:14,525
Saltwater crocodiles get the
same case-by-case treatment.

111
00:07:14,525 --> 00:07:18,352
When a nearly three-metre
crocodile turned up near Marina

112
00:07:18,432 --> 00:07:21,779
East Drive in October 2023,
NParks first looked at

113
00:07:21,859 --> 00:07:25,001
relocating it to Sungei
Buloh Wetland Reserve —

114
00:07:25,081 --> 00:07:29,387
already home to twenty crocodiles
and with no room for another —

115
00:07:29,467 --> 00:07:33,100
and only put it down once
that option was closed off.

116
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:37,202
A smaller crocodile spotted
off Sentosa Cove in January

117
00:07:37,282 --> 00:07:40,547
2026 was simply kept
under watch for a week,

118
00:07:40,627 --> 00:07:45,337
with beaches reopened once it
moved on; another spotted off the

119
00:07:45,417 --> 00:07:49,675
same stretch that May was
caught and euthanised in turn.

120
00:07:49,675 --> 00:07:53,857
The clearest sign of how far
the management philosophy

121
00:07:53,937 --> 00:07:57,725
has shifted sits over the
Bukit Timah Expressway,

122
00:07:57,805 --> 00:08:02,225
on almost the same ridge that
killed two tigers in 1896.

123
00:08:02,225 --> 00:08:05,646
The Eco-Link@BKE, a
75-metre vegetated bridge,

124
00:08:05,726 --> 00:08:09,755
was built for no other reason
than to let animals move

125
00:08:09,835 --> 00:08:13,713
between the Bukit Timah and
Central Catchment nature

126
00:08:13,793 --> 00:08:17,750
reserves without having to
cross the highway at all.

127
00:08:17,750 --> 00:08:21,674
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore spent a century

128
00:08:21,754 --> 00:08:25,678
clearing the jungle that made
tigers a genuine daily danger,

129
00:08:25,758 --> 00:08:30,350
and now spends real effort managing
what has grown back in its place —

130
00:08:30,430 --> 00:08:33,900
a boar, a crocodile, a
hornbill, a family of otters.

131
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:38,110
The forest patches that are left,
Bukit Timah and the Central Catchment

132
00:08:38,190 --> 00:08:41,676
chief among them, have become
some of the few places on the

133
00:08:41,756 --> 00:08:45,845
island where the built-up city
recedes, which may be exactly why they

134
00:08:45,925 --> 00:08:49,712
draw a following among Singapore's
own seniors, many of whom now

135
00:08:49,792 --> 00:08:53,821
treat a hike through them as a
regular part of retirement — a quiet,

136
00:08:53,901 --> 00:08:57,950
present-day echo of an island that
was once almost entirely theirs.
